Complete Render PostgreSQL Migration Guide
The goal is:

Old Render DB → pgAdmin backup → New Render DB → pgAdmin restore → Backend points to new DB

Part 1 — Back up the old database
Step 1: Open pgAdmin
Open pgAdmin 4 on your computer.

You should have your old Render database registered under something like:

Servers
└── Render SuperHR
    └── Databases
        └── superhr

If you haven't registered it yet:

Right-click Servers → Register → Server...

Step 2: Configure the old database connection
Under General:

Name:
Render SuperHR

Under Connection:

Host:             YOUR_OLD_RENDER_HOST
Port:             5432
Maintenance DB:   superhr
Username:         YOUR_OLD_USERNAME
Password:         YOUR_OLD_PASSWORD

Under SSL:

SSL mode: Require

Click Save.

Make sure you can expand:

Databases → superhr

Part 2 — Create the backup
Step 3: Start Backup
Right-click the old database:

superhr

Select:

Backup...

Step 4: Choose the backup filename
For example:

C:\Users\YOUR_NAME\Desktop\superhr_backup.dump

On Mac/Linux, you can put it somewhere convenient such as your Desktop.

Step 5: Choose the format
Set:

Format: Custom

This is important because we'll use this backup with pgAdmin's Restore function.

Step 6: Start the backup
Click:

Backup

Wait for it to finish.

You want to see something equivalent to:

Process completed successfully

The file should no longer be 0 KB.

Part 3 — VERIFY the backup
Do not skip this step.

This protects you if something goes wrong with the Render database.

Step 7: Create a temporary local database
In pgAdmin:

Servers
 → PostgreSQL
   → Databases

Right-click Databases.

Choose:

Create → Database

Name it:

superhr_test

Click Save.

Step 8: Restore the backup into the test database
Right-click:

superhr_test

Choose:

Restore...

Select:

superhr_backup.dump

Set:

Format: Custom or tar

Make sure you are restoring schema + data, not data-only.

Click Restore.

Wait for:

Process completed successfully

Step 9: Check the tables
Expand:

superhr_test
 → Schemas
   → public
     → Tables

You should see your application's tables.

For example:

departments
employees
order_notifications
...

Step 10: Check actual data
Right-click an important table:

View/Edit Data → All Rows

Make sure you see real records.

If you can see your old records, the backup is good. ✅

Part 4 — Create the new Render database
Only after the backup has been successfully verified.

Step 11: Go to Render
Open your Render dashboard.

Click:

New + → PostgreSQL

Step 12: Configure the new database
Give it a new name, for example:

superhr_db2

Choose:

Instance Type: Free

Use the same region as your old database if practical.

For example:

Oregon

Then create the database.

Because your workspace can only have one active Free Postgres database, you may need to delete the old Free database before Render lets you create this one.

Make absolutely sure your backup has been verified before deleting the old database.

Part 5 — Connect the new Render database to pgAdmin
Step 13: Get the new database connection information
In Render:

superhr_db2 → Connect

You'll see connection information.

You need:

Host
Port
Database
Username
Password

For pgAdmin, use the External Database URL/connection information.

Step 14: Register the new server in pgAdmin
In pgAdmin:

Servers → Register → Server...

Under General:

Name:
SuperHR New

Under Connection, enter the new database's information:

Host:             [new Render host]
Port:             5432
Maintenance DB:   superhr_db2
Username:         [new username]
Password:         [new password]

Under SSL:

SSL mode: Require

Click Save.

You should now have:

Servers
├── Render SuperHR
│   └── Databases
│       └── superhr
│
└── SuperHR New
    └── Databases
        └── superhr_db2

Part 6 — Restore the database into the new Render DB
There are two things to restore:

Structure/schema — tables, indexes, sequences, constraints, etc.
Data — the actual records.
Since you're starting with a completely new database, the easiest approach is to restore everything from the backup in one operation.

Step 15: Restore the backup
Right-click:

superhr_db2

Choose:

Restore...

Select:

superhr_backup.dump

Set:

Format: Custom or tar

Step 16: Check restore options
Because this is a brand-new database, you generally want:

Only data:       OFF
Only schema:     OFF
Create database: OFF

You want both schema and data.

If there is an option called:

Clean before restore

you don't need it for an empty new database.

Step 17: Start the restore
Click:

Restore

Wait.

You want:

Process completed successfully

Part 7 — Verify the new database
Step 18: Refresh pgAdmin
Right-click:

superhr_db2

and select:

Refresh

Then expand:

superhr_db2
 → Schemas
   → public
     → Tables

You should see all your tables.

Step 19: Check important tables
Right-click:

departments

Choose:

View/Edit Data → All Rows

Confirm your old department records are there.

Do the same for important tables such as:

employees
orders
order_notifications
users

Use the tables that matter to your application.

Part 8 — Make sure the data is actually there
You can also run SQL in pgAdmin.

Open:

Tools → Query Tool

Select superhr_db2.

Run:

SELECT COUNT(*) FROM departments;

Then:

SELECT COUNT(*) FROM order_notifications;

And for other important tables:

SELECT COUNT(*) FROM employees;

The counts should correspond to what you had in the old database.

Part 9 — Update your backend
Now your new database is ready.

Go to your backend Web Service in Render.

Open:

Environment

Find:

DATABASE_URL

Step 20: Change DATABASE_URL
Replace the old database connection with the new database's Internal Database URL.

Because your backend and PostgreSQL database are both on Render, the Internal Database URL is preferable.

It will look approximately like:

postgresql://username:password@internal-render-host/database

Use the exact Internal Database URL displayed by Render.

Do not paste the password into this chat.

Step 21: Save
Click:

Save Changes

Render should restart/redeploy your backend.

Part 10 — Test your application
Once the backend finishes deploying:

Step 22: Test login
Try logging into your application.

Step 23: Test existing records
Check that old data is present.

For example:

Existing users
Employees
Departments
Orders
Notifications
Step 24: Test creating data
Create a new record.

For example:

Create a new employee

Then refresh the application and make sure it remains there.

Step 25: Check backend logs
In Render:

Backend → Logs

Look for errors such as:

relation does not exist
connection refused
password authentication failed
database does not exist

If there are no database errors and the application works, the migration is successful. ✅

Part 11 — Do NOT immediately delete things
Once your new database works, keep your verified backup:

superhr_backup.dump

Keep it somewhere safe, preferably two locations.

For example:

Desktop/
    superhr_backup.dump

Google Drive/
    superhr_backup.dump

Then you can safely proceed with cleanup.

Part 12 — Security cleanup
Because you posted both database credentials earlier:

Step 26: Rotate the database password
In Render, change/rotate the password for the new PostgreSQL database.

Then update your backend's:

DATABASE_URL

with the new credential.

Redeploy/restart the backend and verify that it still connects.

Your final setup
When everything is finished, it should look like:

                 ┌─────────────────────┐
                 │   Your Frontend     │
                 └──────────┬──────────┘
                            │
                            ▼
                 ┌─────────────────────┐
                 │   Render Backend    │
                 │                     │
                 │ DATABASE_URL = NEW  │
                 └──────────┬──────────┘
                            │
                            ▼
                 ┌─────────────────────┐
                 │   superhr_db2       │
                 │   Free PostgreSQL   │
                 │                     │
                 │ Tables + ALL DATA   │
                 └─────────────────────┘

                 Backup kept separately:
                 superhr_backup.dump

The critical rule
Never delete the old database until this sequence is complete:

Backup
   ↓
Restore to test database
   ↓
Verify tables
   ↓
Verify actual data
   ↓
Create new Render DB
   ↓
Restore backup
   ↓
Verify new DB
   ↓
Update backend DATABASE_URL
   ↓
Test application
   ↓
Rotate password
   ↓
Only then clean up old resources

This gives you a reliable procedure you can repeat whenever you need to migrate the Free Render PostgreSQL database